reason=Incident label events reconcile CI mode. New .github/workflows/incident-label-ci.yml (152 lines) acts on a labeled/unlabeled event for expedited-incident-response ONLY, on a same-repo PR. It selects the newest pull_request ci.yml run of that PR (by number) on its current head; with none listed yet it stops green. Otherwise it at once creates an in-progress ci-green check run (a hold) as the GitHub Actions app, whose ci-green the merge ruleset requires, so nothing merges on the stale verdict, including after a COMPLETED expedited run. It stops an unfinished run (the cancel retried until accepted, then waited for), re-runs it, and keeps the hold until the re-run has COMPLETED (bounded, within a 250-minute job timeout). Then it releases as neutral every hold of that label on the head up to its own, and the re-run's own ci-green decides. Measured on livespec-dev-tooling#3911: released any earlier, the head read MERGEABLE while the re-run was pending; never released, it stayed BLOCKED after the full run passed. Its concurrency group is shared only by incident label events. auto-enable-merge.yml gains one gate conjunct plus 4 comment lines, inserted with no existing line changed: it skips the unlabeled event of that label, so it cannot race the hold. That only narrows when auto-merge arms. Any other label skips the new job at the job level (no runner), and ci.yml's triggers are unchanged. An incident label event costs one ubuntu-latest job that waits out one CI re-run, plus that re-run. 157 lines added, 0 removed or modified against merge base 1647255b (re-measured after the rebase onto master); no check removed; the required-check set unchanged. The reusable-check-matrix omission half was split out to livespec-dev-tooling-hlxbsv: it reduces a quality gate (maintainer tier).
